{
  "case_id": "2006-05-998",
  "document_hash": "5cf30b83a25a7f3e4053def8301bd305fcf011edef7d5654c1eedcbf714d1d23",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2006-05-998",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5cf30b83a25a7f3e4053def8301bd305fcf011edef7d5654c1eedcbf714d1d23",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5cf30b83a25a-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In Neder versus United States, this Court held that the omission of an offense element from petit jury instructions can be harmless error, even though that omission violates the Sixth Amendment right to a jury trial. Our submission is that the same analysis applies to the omission of an offense element from a grand jury's indictment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2006-05-998",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5cf30b83a25a7f3e4053def8301bd305fcf011edef7d5654c1eedcbf714d1d23",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5cf30b83a25a-0000-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The motion was erroneously denied by the trial judge."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2006-05-998",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5cf30b83a25a7f3e4053def8301bd305fcf011edef7d5654c1eedcbf714d1d23",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5cf30b83a25a-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "How far would you go with the Mechanik logic? Suppose that someone is charged by information with a felony without the person's consent and for some reason the trial judge refuses to dismiss the information and then the person is convicted. Would you say that because the petit jury returned a verdict that the fact that the person was charged with a felony by information calls for no remedy?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Dreeben",
      "case_id": "2006-05-998",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5cf30b83a25a7f3e4053def8301bd305fcf011edef7d5654c1eedcbf714d1d23",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5cf30b83a25a-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "No, I would not go that far, Justice Alito. I would draw the same line that this Court drew in the Midland Asphalt case where it was"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2006-05-998",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5cf30b83a25a7f3e4053def8301bd305fcf011edef7d5654c1eedcbf714d1d23",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5cf30b83a25a-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So it's just a metaphysical inquiry, when is an indictment not an indictment? It's not some other standard of what's fundamentally unfair or, from the Sixth amendment, whether or not there's notice?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Dreeben",
      "case_id": "2006-05-998",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5cf30b83a25a7f3e4053def8301bd305fcf011edef7d5654c1eedcbf714d1d23",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5cf30b83a25a-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And I would submit that the same kind of principle would apply here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Baggot",
      "case_id": "2006-05-998",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5cf30b83a25a7f3e4053def8301bd305fcf011edef7d5654c1eedcbf714d1d23",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5cf30b83a25a-0001-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Your Honor, if there is a clerical error, Rule 36 gives the trial judge the ability, and the power, and the authority to correct an error which is purely clerical. I refer you to the case of Contreras-Rojas, which was this exact defense."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-05-998",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "5cf30b83a25a7f3e4053def8301bd305fcf011edef7d5654c1eedcbf714d1d23",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5cf30b83a25a-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Does the Government have to pick one overt act and say that's it, when it introduces evidence of a whole string of them?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2006-05-998",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "5cf30b83a25a7f3e4053def8301bd305fcf011edef7d5654c1eedcbf714d1d23",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5cf30b83a25a-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, I don't see how we can really address it unless they tell us what they're talking about. And I would point out as of today the Government has still not identified a single act that is their overt act."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-05-998",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "5cf30b83a25a7f3e4053def8301bd305fcf011edef7d5654c1eedcbf714d1d23",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5cf30b83a25a-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "May I ask this question. Should the test for the missing element be different for the test for the wrong element? In other words, suppose the indictment alleged he walked up to the border and the evidence showed he rode a bicycle. There's a variance. Would that present a different legal issue than if they just leave the overt act entirely out?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2006-05-998",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "5cf30b83a25a7f3e4053def8301bd305fcf011edef7d5654c1eedcbf714d1d23",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5cf30b83a25a-0002-0007",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "(Whereupon, at 11:01 a.m., the case in the above-entitled matter was submitted.)"
    },
